{"evt":"summary_set","id":"note:note-mqpxyj4af7t","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","summary":"Capstone fair comparison: all arms run in locate-then-fetch mode delivering ACTUAL code (same ~12k char budget, identical get_code_snippet fetch step, so only the LOCATE step differs). Over the 13-query corpus and the 863-note (65 percent) cmm-onboarded KB: subconscious @ cmm-KB (locate via /search, fetch snippet) mean_recall 0.513, mean_tokens 1314, mean_tok_per_correct 608, hit 9/13. codebase-memory-mcp (locate via search_graph, fetch snippet) 0.551 recall, 2554 tokens, 1113 tok/correct, 9/13. naive (grep+read) 0.321, 3026, 1945, 7/13. CONCLUSION: once code is onboarded into the KB, the subconscious agentic search LOCATES as well as cmm native structural query (0.513 vs 0.551, within ~7 percent) and delivers it at FEWER tokens (1314 vs 2554; 608 vs 1113 tok/correct) — the KB semantic recall pulled a tighter, more-targeted set of functions. Both crush naive. Validates the architecture: cmm (or a first-party AST extractor) onboards code structure into the KB, and the orchestrator's own retrieval is competitive-with / more-efficient-than cmm's native query on a fair deliver-code basis. Caveats: 65 percent partial KB (full onboard, now enabled by the batch-ingest refactor, would firm it up); n=13 so the subconscious-beats-cmm tok/correct ordering is within noise, but competitive-and-efficient is robust; the 4 zero-recall queries (q05 computeFlow, q06 callsAvoided, q10, q13) are coverage/ranking gaps. Methodology note: get_code_snippet requires qualified_name (resolve name->qualified_name via search_graph first); fetching a function body also surfaces neighbor symbols, lifting recall above the locators-only numbers.","actor":"overlay-sync","ts":"2026-06-23T02:28:46.848Z"}
